#### Writing a Function Handler (Ruby runtime)

```ruby
# src/main.rb: Appwrite Function entry point
def main(context)
    # context.req.body:         raw body (String)
    # context.req.body_json:    parsed JSON (Hash or nil)
    # context.req.headers:      headers (Hash)
    # context.req.method:       HTTP method
    # context.req.path:         URL path
    # context.req.query:        query params (Hash)

    context.log("Processing: #{context.req.method} #{context.req.path}")

    if context.req.method == 'GET'
        return context.res.json({ message: 'Hello from Appwrite Function!' })
    end

    context.res.json({ success: true })          # JSON
    # context.res.text('Hello')                  # plain text
    # context.res.empty                          # 204
    # context.res.redirect('https://...')         # 302
end
```

### Server-Side Rendering (SSR) Authentication

SSR apps using Ruby frameworks (Rails, Sinatra, etc.) use the **server SDK** to handle auth. You need two clients:

- **Admin client**: uses an API key, creates sessions, bypasses rate limits (reusable singleton)
- **Session client**: uses a session cookie, acts on behalf of a user (create per-request, never share)

```ruby
require '{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}'
include {{ spec.info.title }}

# Admin client (reusable)
admin_client = Client.new
    .set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .set_project('[PROJECT_ID]')
    .set_key(ENV['{{ spec.info.title | caseUpper }}_API_KEY'])

# Session client (create per-request)
session_client = Client.new
    .set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .set_project('[PROJECT_ID]')

session = cookies['a_session_[PROJECT_ID]']
session_client.set_session(session) if session
```

#### Email/Password Login (Sinatra)

```ruby
post '/login' do
    account = Account.new(admin_client)
    session = account.create_email_password_session(
        email: params[:email],
        password: params[:password]
    )

    # Cookie name must be a_session_<PROJECT_ID>
    response.set_cookie('a_session_[PROJECT_ID]', {
        value: session.secret,
        httponly: true,
        secure: true,
        same_site: :strict,
        path: '/',
    })

    content_type :json
    { success: true }.to_json
end
```

#### Authenticated Requests

```ruby
get '/user' do
    session = request.cookies['a_session_[PROJECT_ID]']
    halt 401, { error: 'Unauthorized' }.to_json unless session

    session_client = Client.new
        .set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
        .set_project('[PROJECT_ID]')
        .set_session(session)

    account = Account.new(session_client)
    user = account.get

    content_type :json
    user.to_json
end
```

#### OAuth2 SSR Flow

```ruby
# Step 1: Redirect to OAuth provider
get '/oauth' do
    account = Account.new(admin_client)
    redirect_url = account.create_o_auth2_token(
        provider: OAuthProvider::GITHUB,
        success: 'https://example.com/oauth/success',
        failure: 'https://example.com/oauth/failure'
    )
    redirect redirect_url
end

# Step 2: Handle the callback and exchange the token for a session
get '/oauth/success' do
    account = Account.new(admin_client)
    session = account.create_session(
        user_id: params[:userId],
        secret: params[:secret]
    )

    response.set_cookie('a_session_[PROJECT_ID]', {
        value: session.secret,
        httponly: true, secure: true, same_site: :strict, path: '/',
    })

    content_type :json
    { success: true }.to_json
end
```

> **Cookie security:** Always use `httponly`, `secure`, and `same_site: :strict` to prevent XSS. The cookie name must be `a_session_<PROJECT_ID>`.

> **Forwarding user agent:** Call `session_client.set_forwarded_user_agent(request.user_agent)` to record the end-user's browser info for debugging and security.

## Error Handling

```ruby
require '{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}'
include {{ spec.info.title }}

begin
    row = tables_db.get_row(database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]', table_id: '[TABLE_ID]', row_id: '[ROW_ID]')
rescue {{ spec.info.title }}::Exception => e
    puts e.message    # human-readable message
    puts e.code       # HTTP status code (Integer)
    puts e.type       # error type (e.g. 'document_not_found')
    puts e.response   # full response body (Hash)
end
```

**Common error codes:**

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `401` | Unauthorized: missing or invalid session/API key |
| `403` | Forbidden: insufficient permissions |
| `404` | Not found: resource does not exist |
| `409` | Conflict: duplicate ID or unique constraint |
| `429` | Rate limited: too many requests |

## Permissions & Roles (Critical)

Appwrite uses permission strings to control access to resources. Each permission pairs an action (`read`, `update`, `delete`, `create`, or `write` which grants create + update + delete) with a role target. By default, **no user has access** unless permissions are explicitly set at the row/file level or inherited from the table/bucket settings. Permissions are arrays of strings built with the `Permission` and `Role` helpers.

```ruby
# Permission and Role are included in the main require
require '{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}'
include {{ spec.info.title }}
```

### Database Row with Permissions

```ruby
doc = tables_db.create_row(
    database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    table_id: '[TABLE_ID]',
    row_id: ID.unique,
    data: { title: 'Hello World' },
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),     # specific user can read
        Permission.update(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),   # specific user can update
        Permission.read(Role.team('[TEAM_ID]')),     # all team members can read
        Permission.read(Role.any),                   # anyone (including guests) can read
    ]
)
```

### File Upload with Permissions

```ruby
file = storage.create_file(
    bucket_id: '[BUCKET_ID]',
    file_id: ID.unique,
    file: InputFile.from_path('/path/to/file.png'),
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.any),
        Permission.update(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),
        Permission.delete(Role.user('[USER_ID]')),
    ]
)
```

> **When to set permissions:** Set row/file-level permissions when you need per-resource access control. If all rows in a table share the same rules, configure permissions at the table/bucket level and leave row permissions empty.

> **Common mistakes:**
> - **Forgetting permissions**: the resource becomes inaccessible to all users (including the creator)
> - **`Role.any` with `write`/`update`/`delete`**: allows any user, including unauthenticated guests, to modify or remove the resource
> - **`Permission.read(Role.any)` on sensitive data**: makes the resource publicly readable

## Multi-tenancy with Teams

Model each tenant (an organization, workspace, school, or customer account) as a **team**. Memberships carry the tenant roles, and `Role.team` permissions keep one tenant's rows and files invisible to every other tenant. All tenants share one project and one set of tables.

| Tenant concept | {{ spec.info.title }} primitive |
|----------------|---------------------|
| Tenant | Team (the team ID is the tenant ID) |
| User belongs to a tenant | Membership (`confirm` is `true` once joined) |
| Tenant roles (`owner`, `admin`, `member`) | Membership `roles` (free-form strings) |
| Tenant-only data | Row/file permissions with `Role.team(tenant_id)` or `Role.team(tenant_id, 'admin')` |
| Tenant settings (plan, branding) | Team prefs (`get_prefs` / `update_prefs`). Every member can read them, so never store secrets there |
| Platform staff across all tenants | A user label set with `users.update_labels`, granted through `Role.label('staff')` |

### Configure Tenant Tables (once)

Turn on row security and keep table-level permissions to `create`. Table-level permissions apply to **every row**, and a user needs access at the table level *or* the row level, so `Permission.read(Role.users)` on the table lets every signed-in user read every tenant's rows.

```ruby
require '{{ spec.info.title | caseLower }}'
include {{ spec.info.title }}
include {{ spec.info.title }}::Enums

tables_db = TablesDB.new(admin_client) # API key client

tables_db.create_table(
    database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    table_id: 'invoices',
    name: 'Invoices',
    permissions: [Permission.create(Role.users)],  # signed-in users may create rows; nothing else table-wide
    row_security: true,                            # row permissions decide who sees each row
    columns: [
        { key: 'tenantId', type: 'varchar', size: 36,  required: true },
        { key: 'title',    type: 'varchar', size: 255, required: true },
        { key: 'amount',   type: 'double',             required: true },
    ]
)

# Index tenantId so per-tenant filters stay fast (the column must be available first)
tables_db.create_index(
    database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    table_id: 'invoices',
    key: 'tenant_idx',
    type: TablesDBIndexType::KEY,
    columns: ['tenantId']
)
```

If only server code creates rows, leave the table-level `permissions` empty.

### Create a Tenant

With an API key, `create` makes a team with **no members**. Add the owner yourself:

```ruby
admin_teams = Teams.new(admin_client)

tenant = admin_teams.create(team_id: ID.unique, name: 'Example Corp')
admin_teams.create_membership(
    team_id: tenant.id,
    roles: ['owner', 'admin'],
    user_id: '[USER_ID]'         # with an API key the user joins at once; no invite email
)
```

Calling `create` through a user's session client instead makes that user the owner, and `roles` sets the **creator's** roles (default `['owner']`).

> **Teams have no fixed role list.** A role exists as soon as a membership carries it. Roles are flat strings: `owner` does not imply `admin`. Only `owner` is special: owners invite and remove members, change roles, update the team, and delete it.

### Invite and Manage Members

Which client calls `create_membership` decides what happens:

- **Session client** (acting as a tenant owner): sends an invite email and creates a pending membership (`confirm` is `false`) until the user accepts.
- **Admin client** (API key): sends no email and adds the user immediately. Use it for SSO provisioning, bulk imports, or a custom invite flow.

```ruby
# As the signed-in owner: send an invite email
teams = Teams.new(session_client)
teams.create_membership(
    team_id: '[TENANT_ID]',
    roles: ['member'],
    email: 'user@example.com',
    url: 'https://example.com/accept-invite'   # hostname must be registered as a platform
)

# /accept-invite route: {{ spec.info.title }} appended teamId, membershipId, userId, and secret to the url.
# No session is needed: the secret authorizes the accept.
teams.update_membership_status(
    team_id: params['teamId'],
    membership_id: params['membershipId'],
    user_id: params['userId'],
    secret: params['secret']
)

# Members of a tenant
members = teams.list_memberships(team_id: '[TENANT_ID]')

# One user's membership and roles in a tenant
mine = teams.list_memberships(
    team_id: '[TENANT_ID]',
    queries: [Query.equal('userId', '[USER_ID]')]
)

# Change a member's roles (owners only, or an API key)
teams.update_membership(team_id: '[TENANT_ID]', membership_id: '[MEMBERSHIP_ID]', roles: ['admin'])

# Remove a member, revoke a pending invite, or leave the tenant (users can delete their own membership)
teams.delete_membership(team_id: '[TENANT_ID]', membership_id: '[MEMBERSHIP_ID]')
```

Deleting a membership revokes access at once: the user stops matching every `Role.team` permission for that tenant. To hide member names, emails, and MFA status from other members, enable membership privacy in the Console under **Auth** > **Policies** > **Memberships**.

### Write and Read Tenant Data

Use the per-request session client so permissions apply:

```ruby
tables_db = TablesDB.new(session_client)

# Tag every row with its tenant and grant access through the tenant's team
tables_db.create_row(
    database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    table_id: 'invoices',
    row_id: ID.unique,
    data: { tenantId: '[TENANT_ID]', title: 'Q2 Invoice', amount: 2500 },
    permissions: [
        Permission.read(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]')),             # every member of the tenant
        Permission.update(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]', 'admin')),  # tenant admins only
        Permission.delete(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]', 'admin')),
    ]
)

# Returns only rows the user can read, from ALL of the user's tenants.
# Filter by tenantId to show the active tenant.
invoices = tables_db.list_rows(
    database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    table_id: 'invoices',
    queries: [Query.equal('tenantId', '[TENANT_ID]')]
)
```

> **A session or JWT client can only grant roles the user holds.** A user whose only role is `member` gets `401` when setting `Permission.update(Role.team('[TENANT_ID]', 'admin'))`. Grant roles the user has (such as `Role.team('[TENANT_ID]')`), or write the row with the admin client, which can grant any role.

Files follow the same pattern: create the bucket with `file_security: true` and give each file `Role.team` permissions. Realtime needs no extra work: subscribers only receive events for rows and files they can read.

### API Keys Ignore Tenant Boundaries

An API key bypasses all permissions, so the admin client sees every tenant's data. For requests made on behalf of a user:

1. **Act as the user** with the per-request session client from the SSR section, or with a JWT. Permissions then isolate tenants for you.
2. **If you must use the API key**, confirm the caller's membership and roles first, then filter every query by `tenantId`. Take the user ID from the verified session or the `x-appwrite-user-id` function header, never from the request body.

```ruby
# Appwrite Function: act as the calling user
jwt = context.req.headers['x-appwrite-user-jwt']   # present only when a signed-in user called the function
raise 'Unauthorized' if jwt.nil?                   # or return an error response

user_client = Client.new
    .set_endpoint(ENV['APPWRITE_FUNCTION_API_ENDPOINT'])
    .set_project(ENV['APPWRITE_FUNCTION_PROJECT_ID'])
    .set_jwt(jwt)

invoices = TablesDB.new(user_client).list_rows(
    database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    table_id: 'invoices',
    queries: [Query.equal('tenantId', '[TENANT_ID]')]
)

# API key path: verify membership before touching tenant data
def require_tenant_role(admin_teams, tenant_id, user_id, role = nil)
    result = admin_teams.list_memberships(
        team_id: tenant_id,
        queries: [Query.equal('userId', user_id)]
    )
    membership = result.memberships.find(&:confirm)
    raise 'Forbidden' if membership.nil? || (role && !membership.roles.include?(role))

    membership
end
```

### Offboard a Tenant

Deleting a team removes its memberships, not the rows and files that grant it access, and a team created later with the same ID would inherit them. Delete tenant data first, with the admin client, in every tenant table and bucket:

```ruby
TablesDB.new(admin_client).delete_rows(
    database_id: '[DATABASE_ID]',
    table_id: 'invoices',
    queries: [Query.equal('tenantId', '[TENANT_ID]')]
)

admin_teams.delete(team_id: '[TENANT_ID]')
```

### Choosing an Isolation Model

| Model | Use when | Trade-off |
|-------|----------|-----------|
| Shared tables + team permissions (default) | Most SaaS apps | One schema for all tenants; isolation depends on correct permissions |
| Table or database per tenant | Tenants need their own schema, or their data must be exported or dropped as a unit | Every schema change runs once per tenant. Put `Role.team(tenant_id)` in the table-level permissions and leave row security off |
| Project per tenant | Tenants need separate users, auth settings, or regions | Users and sessions cannot cross projects; far more to operate |

> **Common mistakes:**
> - **Table-level `read`/`update`/`delete` for `Role.users` or `Role.any` on a tenant table**: exposes every tenant's rows
> - **Row security left off**: row permissions are ignored and only table-level permissions count
> - **Treating `tenantId` as the security boundary**: it is a filter, and clients can write any value into it. Permissions are the boundary
> - **Serving user requests with the API key and no membership check**: the key sees every tenant
> - **Granting a role the user lacks through a session or JWT client**: fails with `401`; use the admin client instead
> - **Expecting `owner` to imply `admin`**: roles are flat strings; give users every role your permissions check
> - **Deleting a team but not its data**: orphaned rows remain, and a future team with the same ID inherits them

